> 1. Which class do you think is the easiest to play, and why?

The Tourist.

(Waits for gasps of disbelief.)

Why? Because it forces you to play in the most effective way one can
play nethack, that is, defensively and with utmost care. You have no
choice (at the beginning) but to run away from fights and make the most
of your pet and the seemingly useless items you get. This strategy gives
you the best survival rate. (Well, after you find out the hard way how
to survive as a tourist. :-P) This goes on until you complete the Quest,
at which time the tables are turned. Armed with the PYEC, you can
basically squeeze every last drop out of your wands, markers, etc., and
with the Hawaiian shirt to enchant, you can easily get an ascendable
character in no time.  (OK, I exaggerate. But still, you've to admit the
endgame is a lot easier when you don't have to worry (that much) about
how many spare wands/magic marker charges you have.)


> 2. Consider the choice of genociding spellcasters versus powerful
> melee monsters.

Melee monsters are completely worthless to genocide. If you aren't ready
to face, say, minotaurs (a common genocide target, it seems), you've no
business wandering around in Gehennom. Ants, another common genocide
target, are a waste 'cos genociding them is useful only for a short time
at the beginning of the game. (Once you get to the mid-game, ants should
no longer be an issue at all. In fact, they shouldn't be an issue at all
if you're playing properly.) I wouldn't waste a ?oGeno on something that
short-term.

Spellcasters, OTOH, are worth considering as genocide targets.
High-level liches are especially nasty if you don't know how to deal
with them (or are inadequately prepared). I would also add MMF's and
MF's to this list, since a botched encounter with them can easily cause
a lot of subsequent banging of your head on the wall.

Having said all that, my personal opinion is that genocide should only
be used sparingly, when your hand is forced, or preferably not at all.
(Genoless conduct is default for me.) Learning to deal with nasty,
troublesome monsters is part of learning how to play effectively.


